```json
[
 {
  "id": "F1",
  "file": "src/workers/client.ts",
  "line": 33,
  "title": "Pairing can't detect a relay between worker and coordinator",
  "detail": "The worker accepts any certificate on its first request, prints the code the server returned, and pins whatever fingerprint it observed. The displayed code is chosen by the server and is not bound to either TLS endpoint, so comparing the two codes cannot show that the worker reached a different machine. Reproduction (Codex): two loopback TLS listeners, one relaying /worker/pair and /worker/pair/claim to the coordinator. Both displayed codes matched, pairWorker saved the relay's fingerprint instead of the coordinator's, and the token the relay received then authenticated to the coordinator. Fix: derive the displayed code independently on each side from values each side observed: the coordinator from its own certificate fingerprint, the worker fingerprint in the request and a server nonce; the worker from the coordinator fingerprint it observed on the TLS socket, its own fingerprint and the nonce from the reply. The worker must print its locally derived code, never a code from the response. In the same probe, independently derived codes differed through the relay. Add a test with an in-process relay showing the codes differ, and one showing they match on a direct connection."
 },
 {
  "id": "F2",
  "file": "src/cli/daemon.ts",
  "line": 30,
  "title": "The coordinator key and worker credentials are read-denied only while a listener or worker process holds them",
  "detail": "protectPrivateFile runs inside certificate() and privateFile(), so a path is protected only while that call's holder is alive. startWorkerListener returns before certificate() when [workers] listen is unset, and the worker files are protected only inside the `limitless worker` process. Its registrations don't reach agents launched by a daemon on the same machine, and runWorker never protects worker/key.pem. The API token (src/server/api-token.ts) and the database (src/db/store.ts) are protected for the daemon's whole lifetime. Reproduction (Codex, real Seatbelt): start and stop the listener, then start with listen unset; an editor profile built through homeReadPolicy could read the retained worker-tls/key.pem, while the active listener's profile denied it. With a worker running against the same data home, sandboxed reads of worker/token, worker/key.pem and worker/coordinator-fingerprint succeeded. Fix: at daemon start, before integrations or runs start and whether or not a listener is configured, protect worker-tls/key.pem, worker/token, worker/key.pem and worker/coordinator-fingerprint, and keep them protected until shutdown. Also protect worker/key.pem in runWorker. Extend the real-Seatbelt test to start the daemon's protection with listen unset and probe all four paths."
 },
 {
  "id": "F3",
  "file": "src/db/workers.ts",
  "line": 54,
  "title": "The pairing claim secret is not registered for redaction",
  "detail": "The 64-hex claim secret lets its holder collect the worker's token once the pairing is approved, but neither side registers it, so redactCredentials leaves it unchanged in logs, errors and events. Reproduction (Codex): complete a pairing, then pass its secret through redactCredentials; it comes back unchanged, while the issued token is redacted. Fix: registerCredential for the secret on the coordinator when it is issued and on the worker when it is received. The short pairing code is meant to be displayed and can stay in its two intended displays."
 },
